BT /F1 10 Tf 36 756 Td (Asteron Ledger is a fictional manufacturer of industrial sensors serving regional utilities and logistics operators. Revenue reached 248 million credits in fiscal 2025, compared with 219 million credits in 2024. Growth came from higher unit shipments, service renewals, and a modest price increase. The industrial sensing division contributed 156 million credits, while monitoring software contributed 62 million credits and maintenance contributed 30 million credits. Management expects measured expansion, but these figures are illustrative and not investment advice.) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
9 0 obj
<< /Length 607 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 36 756 Td (Liquidity remained adequate throughout fiscal 2025. Asteron Ledger ended the year with 74 million credits of cash and equivalents, compared with 61 million credits one year earlier. Operating cash flow was 43 million credits after capital spending of 18 million credits. The company maintains an undrawn revolving facility of 90 million credits with a fictional community bank. Current assets were 142 million credits and current liabilities were 97 million credits. Management reviews liquidity weekly and keeps a reserve for warranty claims and seasonal inventory purchases.) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
10 0 obj
<< /Length 618 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 36 756 Td (Debt obligations totaled 118 million credits at year end, including 70 million credits of term loans and 48 million credits of equipment financing. The weighted average interest rate was 5.4 percent, and scheduled principal payments are 16 million credits during 2026. Asteron Ledger reported an interest coverage ratio of 6.1 times under its internal calculation. Loan agreements include customary reporting requirements and a leverage covenant. The company has no foreign currency debt in this fictional example, although refinancing costs and rate changes could affect future results.) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
11 0 obj
<< /Length 624 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 36 756 Td (Supplier risk remains a material operating consideration. The largest sensor component supplier provided 34 percent of annual component purchases, while the five largest suppliers provided 68 percent. Management is qualifying a second source for ceramic housings and plans to hold additional safety stock. Raw material prices for copper and specialty polymers fluctuated during the year. A six week disruption at one supplier could delay selected customer orders. These fictional risk figures support analysis practice only and do not describe a real company or recommend any financial action.) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
